ntrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fiat%2FCJ_CTc3c6yNY90DB6dT79PoI-odaCqYFj7ce1gwUE9I.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fiat%2FCJ_CTc3c6yNY90DB6dT79PoI-odaCqYFj7ce1gwUE9I.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>","options":["$\\mathbf{N}, \\mathbf{O}$ and $\\mathbf{P}$ only","$\\mathbf{M}, \\mathbf{O}$ and $\\mathbf{P}$ only","$\\mathbf{N}$ and $\\mathbf{P}$ only","$\\mathbf{N}$ and $\\mathbf{O}$ only"],"answer":2,"solution":"No solution available. ","difficulty":"Easy","source":"IAT (IISER) 2022","_marksId":"67fdff68378e1624ea7b6b19"},{"id":24044,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes","exam":"Engineering","examName":"IAT (IISER)","q":"Which one is an INCORRECT statement with regard to the following reaction?<br><img style=\"width: 500px\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-self-325033b8f02978a5.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-self-325033b8f02978a5.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-self-325033b8f02978a5.png\">\n<img style=\"width: 500px\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fquizrr%2Fquestion-assets%2Fiat_nest%2Fpyq_2026%2Fwatermarked_images%2Fiat_2026_chemistry__q6_d1.png?alt=media\" />","options":["The reaction rate decreases upon changing the solvent from ethyl alcohol to 1:1 mixture of ethyl alcohol and water.","The reaction rate does not change upon increasing the concentration of hydroxide ion.","The rate determining step is the dissociation of <em>tert</em>-butylbromide.","The reaction rate is proportional to the concentration of <em>tert</em>-butylbromide."],"answer":0,"solution":"The given reaction is the nucleophilic substitution of a tertiary alkyl halide (tert-butyl bromide) to form a tertiary alcohol (tert-butyl alcohol).<br><br>Tertiary alkyl halides undergo substitution via the $S_N1$ mechanism due to steric hindrance which prevents the $S_N2$ mechanism, and the formation of a stable tertiary carbocation intermediate.<br><br>The $S_N1$ mechanism proceeds in two steps:<br>Dissociation of tert-butyl bromide to form a tert-butyl carbocation and a bromide ion. This is the slow, rate-determining step.<br>Attack of the nucleophile ($OH^-$) on the carbocation to form the product.<br><br>From this mechanism, we can evaluate the given statements:<br>1. The rate law for this $S_N1$ reaction is Rate = $k[(CH_3)_3C-Br]$. The rate is proportional to the concentration of tert-butyl bromide, making statement (d) correct.<br>2. The rate-determining step is the dissociation of tert-butyl bromide, making statement (c) correct.<br>3. The concentration of the nucleophile ($OH^-$) does not appear in the rate law, so the reaction rate does not change upon increasing its concentration. Statement (b) is correct.<br>4. $S_N1$ reactions are favored by polar protic solvents because they stabilize the carbocation intermediate and the transition state leading to it through solvation. Water is a more polar solvent than ethyl alcohol. Therefore, changing the solvent from pure ethyl alcohol to a 1:1 mixture of ethyl alcohol and water increases the polarity of the solvent system. This increased polarity will stabilize the intermediate ions better, thereby increasing the reaction rate. Statement (a) claims the rate decreases, which is incorrect.<br><br>Answer: The reaction rate decreases upon changing the solvent from ethyl alcohol to 1:1 mixture of ethyl alcohol and water.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"IAT (IISER) 2026","_marksId":"6a4521838c20d3b91d64f965","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":24045,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes","exam":"Engineering","examName":"IAT (IISER)","q":"Among the haloarenes (I